  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Intermittent light rain mixed with snow, mainly\nnorth of Bangor, through Wednesday. Snow accumulations will be \nextremely minor, if any at all.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nAs of 1pm, a band of light precipitation is moving east through\nthe North Woods with light rain/snow mixed. This band of\nprecipitation will continue to move east through Northern Maine\nthis afternoon, with about two hours of light precipitation at \nany one spot. Total precipitation will be just a few hundredths\nof an inch with this, any snow accumulation is not expected as\ntemperatures are just a bit too warm.\n\nAnother round of precipitation is expected this evening,\nprimarily from roughly Greenville east to Millinocket and east \nto around Houlton. Total liquid equivalent precipitation from\nthis should be 0.10 to 0.20 inches. Temperatures will be\nborderline for rain vs snow, and it is a tough call on\nprecipitation type. That said, think that even if snow does\nfall, any accumulations will be extremely light (less than 0.5\ninch snow). The exception is higher terrain, where an inch or\ntwo of snow accumulation is likely. Road travel impacts are not\nexpected.\n\nProbably a lull in precipitation later tonight, but during the\nday Wednesday there should be isolated to scattered shower\nactivity from daytime instability in NW flow aloft. Again,\ngenerally think temperatures will be just a bit too warm for any\nsnow accumulation. Liquid equivalent totals during the day\nWednesday should be less than 0.10 inch except in the few\nheaviest showers. Shower are possible pretty much anywhere, but\nwill be a bit more favored over the North Woods and southeast to\nthe general Millinocket/Sherman/Houlton region.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Potential for coastal storm development later \nin the week. However, significant uncertainty still exists.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure will develop along the Mid-Atlantic coast \nThursday. This initial low will then track northeast Thursday \nnight into the weekend. A second low should then develop Friday \nand also track northeast into Saturday. However, significant \nuncertainty regarding the tracks of these lows still exists. \nPotential impacts from these lows, including precipitation \ntypes/amounts, Thursday night into the weekend will be dependent\non the eventual tracks of these lows. Current (13z 11/10/26) \nNBM snow probabilities limit snowfall totals to Piscataquis \nCounty and just north west of Bangor, with 60-80% chance of at \nleast 1 inch of snow Friday into Saturday. Probability of 0.5 \ninch expands in area north, east, and south with the same \nprobabilities for the same time period as above. Mean ensemble \nguidance indicates similar probabilities for these areas. \nIndividual ensemble members and deterministic guidance, however,\nstill shows wide variation with the track of the low off the \ncoast. Some members have the center of the low closer to the \ncoast, bringing more significant snowfall to parts of the area. \nAnother cluster of members have the low well offshore, limiting \nany precipitation for the area. The exact track of the system \nwill determine how much, if any, cold air moves further south to\nkeep precipitation as snow. Current precipitation totals are \naround 0.5 inches of liquid for the coast, with the exact track \ndetermining exact totals.",
